{"name":"Cola Bubble","hex":"#3c3024","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cola","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The kola plant, genus Cola, famous for its nut, or one of these nuts.","A beverage or a drink made with kola nut flavoring, caramel and carbonated water."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of colon."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cola"},{"term":"bubble","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A spherically contained volume of air or other gas in a liquid, commonly a soapy liquid.","A small spherical cavity in a solid material."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To produce bubbles, to rise up in bubbles (such as in foods cooking or liquids boiling).","To churn or foment, as if wishing to rise to the surface."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bubble"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cola","blocks":[{"text":"Cola is a carbonated soft drink flavored with vanilla, cinnamon, citrus oils, and other flavorings. The name, however, comes from the kola nut, which imbues the beverage with the stimulant caffeine. A stimulating soda using kola appeared circa 1880 in the United States."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Glass_of_Cola.jpg/330px-Glass_of_Cola.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cola"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Berry_cola_stand_Reuilly.jpg/500px-Berry_cola_stand_Reuilly.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Berry_cola_stand_Reuilly.jpg/1280px-Berry_cola_stand_Reuilly.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Berry_cola_stand_Reuilly.jpg/500px-Berry_cola_stand_Reuilly.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thum